        "original_id": "c8a4f63a-6fc2-52ca-8067-c3d92dad6863",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Camassia, Chelone\n- Places: Camas County, Idaho\n- Concepts: plant taxonomy, native range, place naming\n\nFacts:\n- Camassia is a genus of plants in the asparagus family native to Canada and the United States.\n- Chelone is a genus of four species of perennial herbaceous plants native to eastern North America.\n- Camas County, Idaho, is named for the camas root, or Camassia, a lily-like plant with an edible bulb found in the region.\n- Native Americans and settlers used the edible bulb of Camassia as a food source in the Camas County, Idaho, region.",
